   </property>
   <property name="text">
    <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:14pt;&quot;&gt;!Hola! Welcome to our Tequila distillation station!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:14pt;&quot;&gt;Tequila is an alcoholic beverage made from the blue agave plant, primarily grown in the areas surrounding Tequila in Jalisco, Mexico. Unlike other spirits, its production is heavily regulated. Only certain regions in Mexico can produce it legally under the name &amp;quot;tequila,&amp;quot; giving it a similar protection to France's Champagne. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:14pt;&quot;&gt;Tequila has a rich history dating back centuries. The indigenous Aztecs first used agave plants to produce pulque, a milky fermented drink made from its sap, used often for religious and cermonial purposes. When the Spanish arrived in Mexico, they brought with them their knowledge of distillation and applied it in an attempt to make their own version of pulque, eventually creating what is now known as mezcal, a term that refers to all spirits made from agave. Tequila is a specific kind of mezcal exclusively produced from the blue agave and under strict regional guidelines.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:14pt;&quot;&gt;Today, tequila continues to hold deep cultural significance and is widely enjoyed during festivals, family celebrations, and national holidays. Though traditionally consumed neat or with lime and salt, tequila has gained international popularity as the base for many cocktails, like the margarita. Thanks to growing interest in artisanal spirits and Mexican heritage, tequila is recognized around the world as a craft spirit with bold and unique flavors. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
   </property>
